      <p class="top" id="en"><a href="/">← slashkern</a> · <a href="#it" lang="it">Italiano</a> · <a href="#de" lang="de">Deutsch</a> · <a href="#fr" lang="fr">Français</a> · <a href="#nl" lang="nl">Nederlands</a> · <a href="#sv" lang="sv">Svenska</a></p>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <small>Last updated 11 October 2026</small>
      <h2 id="keep">What we keep</h2>
      <small>As of 9 October 2026</small>
      <table>
        <tr><th scope="row">On your phone</th><td>Your answers, drafts, reflections, your Slash card and settings. Nobody else sees them.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What leaves your phone</th><td>What you write to Slash, and a prompt you choose to try on the AI, goes to our AI provider (Anthropic) to get a reply; slashkern doesn’t keep it. Things you share yourself. Your account copy, if you make an account (Supabase, EU). Usage counts, only if you say yes.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Delete it</th><td>Settings › Delete my data removes everything on this phone, your Slash card included. Delete my account also removes your account copy, if you have one.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What a company sees</th><td>Nothing today. Later, only an answer you choose to send for one brand project, never your email; off by default, and saying no changes nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">How your card is made</th><td>From your own taps after each project. No AI scores you.</td></tr>
      </table>
      <h2>What slashkern keeps</h2>
      <p>Without an account, your chosen field, your answers and drafts, your reflections, your chat with the AI assistant and your settings are saved only in this browser on this device (local storage). We cannot see them, except what you send to the AI assistant (see below).</p>
      <h2>If you create an account</h2>
      <p>An account is optional. If you create one, we keep your email address, your first name and a copy of the same trail, so it syncs across your devices. Our account and database provider, Supabase, stores them in the EU (Frankfurt, Germany). Access rules make sure no other user can read them, and we look at them only when needed to run the service. Your password is never stored in readable form, only as a secure hash. Supabase also sends the emails that confirm your address and reset your password. You stay signed in through a token saved in this browser, not a cookie.</p>
      <h2>What is sent</h2>
      <p>Nothing you write is sent anywhere unless you write to the AI assistant (see below), or you choose to share it (for example with "Send my answers to the slashkern team", which opens your own share sheet or copies the text), or you have an account, in which case your trail goes only to your account and the signs you leave are shown, without your name, to other people on the same project (see below). There are no cookies and no advertising, and usage counts stay off unless you say yes (see below). When you write to the AI assistant, your last messages and your chosen field (and, if you tap Ask Slash on a project, that project and the first 400 characters of your draft) are sent to our AI provider, Anthropic, only so it can write the reply. In Prompting projects, if you tap "Try your prompt on the AI", only that prompt is sent to Anthropic's AI to make the reply, and slashkern does not store it. We do not add your name, so avoid typing personal details in the chat. We do not keep these messages on our servers; Anthropic, a US company, may keep them for a limited time under its own terms, and the transfer from the EU relies on the standard contractual clauses in its terms. Our server keeps your IP address in memory for up to a day to limit abuse. If the service cannot be reached, the assistant answers with pre-written questions that run on your device. The hosting provider (Netlify) processes technical data such as your IP address to deliver the page, as every website host does.</p>
      <h2>Voice and the AI first version</h2>
      <p>Voice: if you tap the microphone in a text box, your browser turns your voice into text. Depending on your browser, the audio may go to its maker (Google or Apple), under that company's own terms. slashkern never records or stores audio. You can also type, or use the microphone on your keyboard. The first version with AI: only if you tap "Make a first version with AI", the project's title, your own idea and answers (and the assistant's questions you answered, if any) are sent to our AI provider, Anthropic, only to write that version. slashkern stores and logs nothing of it. The result stays on your device (and in your account, if you have one) only if you choose "Use this as my project", and it is always marked "Made with AI help". Read aloud (the Listen button) uses your browser's own speech voice and sends nothing to slashkern.</p>
      <h2>Sending an answer to a company</h2>
      <p><b>Status:</b> not active yet. Brand projects are samples for now, and no answer goes to any company.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>How it will work:</b> on a brand project, you can choose to send that one answer to the company behind it. You decide each time, project by project. It is off by default: nothing is sent unless you send it yourself.</li>
        <li><b>What is sent:</b> only the answer you chose to send. Never your first name, your email, your password, your other answers, your reflections, your Slash card, your chat with the AI assistant or your drafts.</li>
        <li><b>Who gets it:</b> only the company behind that project. From then on it is responsible for what it received (an independent controller under the GDPR).</li>
        <li><b>Legal basis:</b> your consent (GDPR Art. 6(1)(a)), given for each answer you send. It is never a condition for using slashkern, and saying no changes nothing about your projects or your Slash card.</li>
        <li><b>Changing your mind:</b> you can stop sending at any time. An answer a company already has is its responsibility; you can ask it to delete it, and we will tell it you asked.</li>
        <li><b>Totals only:</b> a company may also see totals about its own project, for example how many people finished it. Totals are anonymous and cannot be linked back to you.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Tips for the next person</h2>
      <p>After a project you can leave a short tip for the next person (up to 140 characters; the code calls it a "sign"). Without an account it stays on this device. With an account, it is shown to other people who open the same project, together with the date, but never with your name, email or any other detail about you. Tips that contain links, email addresses or long numbers are not shared. Deleting that answer removes its tip; deleting your account removes all of them.</p>
      <h2>Co-op with a friend</h2>
      <p>Two projects in every field can be finished with a friend. When you tap "Finish it with a friend" or "Send to a friend", slashkern builds a link that carries your first name (if you gave one) and your answer (up to 500 characters) after the # sign, and opens your own share sheet or copies the link. The part after # is never sent to our servers, but the app you send the link through can read it, and so can anyone who has the link. Your friend's reply travels back the same way, up to 300 characters with their first name. slashkern keeps both texts only on your device (and in your account, if you have one) so you can read them in slashkern, and removes them with "Delete my data". Nothing is sent unless you choose to send the link.</p>
      <h2>Usage counts, without your name</h2>
      <p><b>Off unless you say yes.</b> slashkern asks once on the Home screen, and you can change your mind any time in Settings. If you say yes, your device makes a random code (not linked to your name, your email or your account) and sends one short line to our database at Supabase (EU, Frankfurt) each time something happens: you said yes, you opened slashkern that day, opened a project, sent an answer, saved a reflection, tapped Share, or asked Slash for help. Each line holds the random code, the kind of event, the field and project number, whether it is a brand project, your language and the time. The random code and the times are still personal data, because they can single out one device over time. That is why this is your choice.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Never sent:</b> your name, your email, anything you write (answers, drafts, reflections, chat), or your account. Your IP address is not stored in our table; Supabase and Netlify process it technically to deliver the request, as any website host does.</li>
        <li><b>Why:</b> to count how many people use slashkern and which projects work, so we can improve it, and to show partners and investors totals, never individuals.</li>
        <li><b>Legal basis:</b> your consent (GDPR Art. 6(1)(a); the random code kept on your device also needs it under the ePrivacy rules). It is never a condition for using slashkern.</li>
        <li><b>Who sees it:</b> the slashkern team. Everyone else gets totals.</li>
        <li><b>How long:</b> at most 12 months, then we delete it.</li>
        <li><b>Switching off and deleting:</b> in Settings, switch off Usage counts: we stop counting and delete what was counted for this device straight away. Delete my data, Log out and Delete my account do the same. If you are offline at that moment, the earlier lines stay until they are deleted at 12 months. The random code is the only link to your device, so if you lose the device we cannot find your lines for you; they go the same way.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Your control</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Export: Settings, then Export my data, downloads your trail as a file: your answers, reflections, drafts, chat and settings. The usage-count choice and its random code are not in it.</li>
        <li>Delete: Settings, then Delete my data, removes it from this device. Clearing your browser data does the same.</li>
        <li>Account: Log out removes your trail from this device and keeps it in your account. Delete my account removes the account and the synced trail for good.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Age</h2>
      <p>slashkern is for people aged 18 and over for now, because it is built for adults and keeps what you write.</p>
      <h2>Not therapy</h2>
      <p>slashkern is not therapy or medical advice. If you are struggling, please talk to someone you trust or a local helpline. If you are in danger, call your local emergency number.</p>
      <h2>Who runs slashkern</h2>
      <p>slashkern is built and run by Manuel Di Paolo (Italy), the data controller. Contact: <a href="mailto:manuel@trykern.it">manuel@trykern.it</a>.</p>
